<BaseLayout title="About | Kenyu F.">
  <article>
    <h1>About</h1>
    
    <p>
      I am a student deeply engaged in mathematics, computer science,
      and linguistics in Japan. This site serves as my digital garden — 
      a quiet space for research notes, formal publications, and ongoing projects.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Research Interests</h2>
      
      <h3>Mathematics</h3>
      <p>
        My interests lie primarily in proof theory and algebra.
        In proof theory, I am studying axiomatic systems,
        model theory, and type theory. In algebra,
        I am especially interested in Cayley–Dickson algebras
        and hyperoperations, with a broader interest in
        the expansion of number systems and algebraic worlds.
      </p>

      <h3>Computer Science</h3>
      <p>
        I am interested in the relationship between computation
        and education. Currently, I am engaged in educational
        research at Kitakyushu City University, particularly
        concerning how computational tools and systems can support
        learning and knowledge transmission.
      </p>

      <h3>Linguistics</h3>
      <p>
        I am learning 20+ languages, including English, Spanish,
        Chinese, Korean, Arabic, Hindi, Persian, Russian,
        and German. My interests extend beyond language acquisition
        itself to multilingual cognition, linguistic structure,
        and cross-linguistic comparison.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Background</h2>
      <p>
        I have been studying Group, Ring, and Field theory independently, 
        exploring the foundational structures of modern algebra.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        If you would like to reach out, you can contact me at 
        <span class="quiet">kenyu.fujimoto [at] icloud.com</span>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
